## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving I/O across tenants is common in cloud environments; scalable, low-latency throttling under bursty workloads is critical for fair resource allocation and performance isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Token bucket per-tenant: enforces long-term rate while allowing short-term bursts
- I/O scheduler integration (WFQ): throttled requests become blocked, enabling fair scheduling across tenants
- Burst handling: burst tolerance B_i absorbs temporary demand spikes
- Starvation prevention: weighted guarantees, timeout thresholds, and deadline-aware throttling ensure no tenant is indefinitely blocked

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t rate;        // bytes per second
    uint64_t burst;       // max bucket capacity
    uint64_t tokens;      // current token count
    uint64_t last_refill; // timestamp of last refill
    spinlock_t lock;
    wait_queue_t waitq;
} tenant_t;

void refill_tokens(tenant_t *t) {
    uint64_t now = get_time_ns();
    uint64_t elapsed = now - t->last_refill;
    uint64_t added = (elapsed * t->rate) / NS_PER_SEC;
    t->tokens = min(t->burst, t->tokens + added);
    t->last_refill = now;
}

bool admit_io(tenant_t *t, size_t sz) {
    bool granted = false;
    spin_lock(&t->lock);
    refill_tokens(t);
    if (t->tokens >= sz) {
        t->tokens -= sz;
        granted = true;
    }
    spin_unlock(&t->lock);
    return granted;
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Single-tenant steady-state**: Verify throughput matches configured rate b_i
2. **Burst test**: Submit requests exceeding rate, confirm burst tolerance absorbs spikes
3. **Multi-tenant fairness**: Run concurrent tenants with different weights, measure bandwidth distribution
4. **Mixed CPU/I/O workload**: Inject CPU-bound processes alongside I/O tenants to detect starvation
5. **Starvation detection**: Enqueue high-priority tenant behind low-priority bulk tenant, verify latency bounds

## Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free design with persistent memory, ensuring visibility, ordering, and crash-safety across architectures.

## Key Concepts
- Seqlocks and lock-free data paths
- Persistent memory semantics and cache-line flushing
- Memory barriers and cross-ISA ordering (x86-64 vs ARM64)
- Crash-consistency guarantees and recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
// Writer (pseudo-C in JS for illustration)
function writeItem(item, payload) {
  item.seq += 1n; // now odd
  item.payload = payload;
  clwb(item.payload_addr); // persist payload
  mfence(); // ensure payload is visible before seq
  item.seq += 1n; // now even
}

function readItem(item) {
  let s1 = item.seq;
  if (s1 % 2n === 1n) return null; // in-progress
  let payload = item.payload;
  let s2 = item.seq;
  if (s1 !== s2) return null; // torn read
  return payload;
}
```

